#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125553} :activity/id #uuid "67f5c7b4-d6b9-56a1-a476-c05d63882f58" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049390 :topic/id #uuid "f0e26f7e-2461-5845-b570-4028c1709f5a" :topic/math-academy-id 1948} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125845 :tutorial/content "Suppose we would like to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2=1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/39/39893af9755488d142116370d451b7a3d92b334c27f983eb302492b0d516c41c.png)\n\nFirst, let's draw our constraint circle ($x^2+y^2=1$) and consider the level curves of $f(x,y).$ These level curves are ellipses of the form\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2 = k.\n$$\n\nAs we increase the parameter $k,$ we get ellipses with larger and larger axes, as shown below. \n\n![](images/d5/d59d566c9cf7e252884a1397eb4ac0c8f85b290790e9063be2ff6eb4a4aff04a.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $k,$ a level curve will touch the circle at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ for some value of $k.$ The value of $k$ corresponding to that level curve gives us the *smallest* possible value of $f(x,y)$ on our circle $x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nIncreasing $k$ even further, we'll get another value of $k$ for which the corresponding level curve touches the circle at the points $(0, \\pm 1),$ as shown below. This value of $k$ gives us the *largest* possible value of the function $f(x,y)$ on our circle.\n\n![](images/48/48f71f5aa237936be24ccba572fb650b0d477c252d8338549a33cfea4874cbad.png)\n\nNotice that the corresponding level curves touch the circle in both cases (the minimum and the maximum). So, the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ and the circle $g(x,y)=1$ have a common tangent at these points. This means that the *gradients* of both curves must be parallel at these points. Therefore, at the extreme points, we must have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is some real number. The number $\\lambda$ is called a **Lagrange multiplier**.\n\nWe can now use these observations to write down a general method for optimizing a function subject to a constraint." :tutorial/id #uuid "932667c3-77f7-56cb-ae08-dd6835e29462" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5109 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "639b5b29-18f2-5454-b193-c76dd6a45f3a" :step/math-academy-id 32055 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124421 :question/id #uuid "dc39fdca-dfbc-5806-b0aa-8480ee44b10f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8689"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523996}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"} :step/id #uuid "ba4c5cbe-9bb4-5323-8a0f-c6fcc06d7cc5" :step/math-academy-id 32291 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124424 :question/id #uuid "ebdcad27-1ee2-57e7-9ebf-29ccf0a8ad12" :question/math-academy-id "e-8736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435021}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"} :step/id #uuid "242a9cf3-c2e5-570a-b9f0-936162411df1" :step/math-academy-id 32494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124208 :question/id #uuid "5c276425-f5ef-585f-9ab3-1b0262fff08e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12456"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483396}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "6e09518a-865f-5121-9684-78bd4bfcab56" :step/math-academy-id 44830 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125872 :tutorial/content "The **method of Lagrange multipliers** states that the extrema of a function $f(x,y)$ subject to constraint $g(x,y)=c$ can be found by solving the system\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curve $g(x,y)=c.$\n\nNote that solutions to this system do not always exist! For example, the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ does not have a minimum (or maximum) on the hyperbola $x^2-y^2=1.$\n\nHowever, we can use the following theorem to guarantee the existence of solutions in some cases:\n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint function $g(x,y) = c$ is closed and bounded, then $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "906426bb-122c-5b43-a304-d63921ab20dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7750 :tutorial/title "The Method of Lagrange Multipliers"} :step/id #uuid "0d14ea94-0983-5096-b7d2-ed0dc8de4dce" :step/math-academy-id 44868 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125894 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider our previous example, where we wish to find the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x^2+4y^2$ on the circle $g(x,y)=x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $2$ at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at points $(0, \\pm 1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a5ffe247-960a-5ed1-92ae-ae8c917cbefc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7881 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "66d693a8-cc66-5185-8b5f-4ba65d4664e2" :step/math-academy-id 45437 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125555}}] :activity/title "Lagrange Multipliers With One Constraint" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124738}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483465} :activity/id #uuid "da0cbfc1-126a-53bb-9e82-05a1649265b0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049390 :topic/id #uuid "f0e26f7e-2461-5845-b570-4028c1709f5a" :topic/math-academy-id 1948} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483437 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c313004-6208-55c4-a29e-409a760d3bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482914} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd23171-d788-5e6e-8ad6-c9430da94769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482952} {:answer/id #uuid "711f158a-fa20-5db5-8546-777a45f7ce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483010} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba5be8a-a984-5074-bd2c-9994fe1ca5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483057} {:answer/id #uuid "d28aa3fe-8784-549c-be59-9299e7bbdf0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483010} :answer-field/id #uuid "110d77ff-11e1-54ff-9518-294a8feb70c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b0386d5f-8012-5e50-aca9-5585df04ede1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162779" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the parabola ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y &=-2\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,-2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\sqrt{2},-1),(\\sqrt{2},-1).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-2),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-2)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1)$ and its minimum value is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "0f702bf3-bb64-5422-aea8-b0bbbff9042c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483444 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf225b47-4c19-5217-8990-347551b6707b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372204} {:answer/id #uuid "56197474-e8f7-5ce5-8d46-2baf9e825918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372205} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2340ab-e19e-52ae-b570-eac281dc9ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372206} {:answer/id #uuid "3454ace2-65ba-5ed8-8b19-9b8ae2dca207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372207} {:answer/id #uuid "60ca4825-53f4-5526-a63c-844efa53d479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372208} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a272c34-6771-5a9a-abbf-859c9c9ddcc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b81c4517-112b-539a-a190-05f9b7ce0159" :question/math-academy-id "q-162774" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=12x+4\\sqrt{6}y\\,$ on the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=6.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨12,4\\sqrt{6}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨6x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12 &=6\\lambda x \\\\ 4\\sqrt{6} &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=6$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{12+12}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\\,(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})\\,$ | $\\,(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-24\\,$ | $\\,24\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $24.$"} :step/id #uuid "1f621894-6c34-5b5d-bbbc-0b80620d0a44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483477}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483465 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112e5b58-a689-5138-b33f-e47c56e7167c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482839} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc280e-d5f1-5a84-9cfc-9c3ad50813fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482920} {:answer/id #uuid "7228d70a-0771-5134-8636-ee3ba6b53f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483011} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e6b23f-d37b-563e-840d-2a93839faacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483179} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bafb54-6e90-57a9-943c-bbd0971097f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482839} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d3adf1-3aa8-506a-a4e2-98cf256eb5b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4126f1c5-7567-585d-9e49-d37c72e8e18c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162753" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4x-2y+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,1),\\,(2,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(2,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-9$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$"} :step/id #uuid "61c5b431-d83c-57cb-a714-7721ae5e576f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483508}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483477 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077e60b6-8dfd-5425-af73-cfd611ef7918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482822} {:answer/id #uuid "53ae6944-c0ae-5fb2-bd44-030a207fcefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482958} {:answer/id #uuid "70f63dec-0347-57c6-8669-f185a64af767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483009} {:answer/id #uuid "e12ede27-37da-589d-8cba-76441bf6394f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483192} {:answer/id #uuid "fde02425-ec57-5a63-be2e-568b6cad03db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5+\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483009} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db7cbdb-ccee-5e24-89d3-8d2ca2dc081d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27a9cc77-ffaa-5079-b6d3-f595b1244e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162776" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10x-2\\sqrt{15}y\\,$ on the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,-2\\sqrt{15}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨10x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}10 &=10\\lambda x \\\\ -2\\sqrt{15} &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5+15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-20\\,$ | $\\,20\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-20.$"} :step/id #uuid "76c5427c-e22a-57a5-a05e-2677ada8f489" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483437}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f53dcb-0e32-5ceb-8aaf-a210c8dc8521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278823} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9bf5ef-0b2f-58e3-a02b-38b0b94d9f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278848} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f417c7-1adf-5968-be5e-7a28cd0f3ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280219} {:answer/id #uuid "c67face3-ea0f-54ec-abf8-31d98bb676cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280331} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e1ed97-fd98-5ee9-abbe-fa7c46a9977b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278823} :answer-field/id #uuid "40847485-fda7-51d7-b82e-0e2ddf1864ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "932d87ff-4a97-5b0b-b8d7-da2ba43e7f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162763" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4y-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,4-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4-2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2) &=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\sqrt{5}),(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-2+1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(\\pm 1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-\\sqrt{5})$ | $(0,\\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 1,-2)$ and its minimum value is $-14.$"} :step/id #uuid "fa39b7f6-ab97-5c13-a5d4-408471cf2b34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483444}}] :activity/title "Lagrange Multipliers With One Constraint" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482810}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524047} :activity/id #uuid "a8eccd29-e4a5-58ea-84f2-d615280c153b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049390 :topic/id #uuid "f0e26f7e-2461-5845-b570-4028c1709f5a" :topic/math-academy-id 1948} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c1410e0-a84a-59db-9179-4e96144445b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361250} {:answer/id #uuid "84d79ecf-1f02-5b4a-8561-b6bcc9948bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361429} {:answer/id #uuid "98291461-add9-53ed-afd7-b0c2bd00a5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361514} {:answer/id #uuid "f250f6bc-6c44-51cf-a2c2-612e5c87a588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361917} {:answer/id #uuid "f466069f-33e0-5fe4-9e0d-eb960bf7ce61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361429} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b70f22b-a5a8-585e-b3dd-fa50d2c97d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8339a664-cc92-5ae4-a4f3-d6ab19787d80" :question/math-academy-id "q-99027" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=1-x-y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,1+\\sqrt{2}\\,$ | $\\,1-\\sqrt{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $1+\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "277fd1b8-f855-56f4-9b8a-ec30a076c788" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85aec1e2-53f5-54ed-8559-44e3da4b0d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121512} {:answer/id #uuid "99d569fc-8fe9-5167-9d86-bcf52cecd477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121513} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e16a3a-d004-5266-9e0c-9d803e28d5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121514} {:answer/id #uuid "023cbcbf-45c4-50e3-8d3e-02f1bd461815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121515} {:answer/id #uuid "51149a6f-1f12-5442-ad22-2b382a3caba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121516} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab1c0d2-9899-5cea-b60d-8ed13448eab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "319e140b-45eb-5dbd-91e1-7f34e0399c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-162795" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y\\,$ on the parabola $y-{x}^{2}=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y-{x}^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=-2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ \\lambda &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(-1,1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens upwards, there isn't a maximum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the minimum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,1),$ and its minimum value is $-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "48ac9f84-9cc5-5dd0-b18e-283909b67aa0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e57bce-4288-5fe3-aa0a-73628c52743d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278746} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3b843e-b72a-51a2-a995-da57e46e487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279446} {:answer/id #uuid "81d5aaa0-fd9f-568f-9bb1-e082fcdaa7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279754} {:answer/id #uuid "a999c752-b365-5d3b-960d-66d2a2fbec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280097} {:answer/id #uuid "ced37650-ae67-5a4c-bc4a-bb74c5223c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279754} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be271dc-3c10-5641-8030-505366b398d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "76775e9c-a3b9-5240-b988-cfc2af339188" :question/math-academy-id "q-162764" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2+1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0),(-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-3$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 2)$ and its minimum value is $-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "65849aaa-3c3d-5a0b-9d2c-50bf3309805a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524057}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8f830f-22a4-5f79-abbe-9554be0e81d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523344} {:answer/id #uuid "43e96650-8ca0-524b-8e9f-31437aefa7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523394} {:answer/id #uuid "4bee1f98-fa33-5093-87bc-2f00c2477543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523408} {:answer/id #uuid "c43a5edc-f944-5fca-9515-c95ccf41820f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523663} {:answer/id #uuid "c8eb3b1c-5d9a-5384-8da8-9f595ee3a2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523674} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc58a902-969b-5c49-b676-fba60e52b489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a3c769ab-089f-58c3-aaa9-e70d964ccd5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162798" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x+y\\,$ on the parabola $y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-2),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda (x-2) \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }+2 \\\\ \\lambda &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{(\\frac{5}{2}-2)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens downwards, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{9}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "87ea2bee-8725-54fe-b713-4f1f542f808d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75b438b0-5bc9-5848-8c09-4b9737f5876d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361357} {:answer/id #uuid "a6441485-d04f-586c-8c4c-ff98f01eb432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361581} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1f6919-eef4-5eeb-be4a-38877febdda3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361671} {:answer/id #uuid "e75d0f27-b8bb-5a5c-8905-034c15c6f3c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361870} {:answer/id #uuid "faf4cd0b-f3f9-5c75-a7ea-835686f7d019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361959}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361959} :answer-field/id #uuid "314fa88d-8f92-5297-95d3-76a7ddb9d25f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47aa66c7-074f-539e-9248-f6b2a1659ce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-99030" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=3x-y+6\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=10\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=10$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-3,1),\\,(3,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-3,1)\\,$ | $\\,(3,-1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,16\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(3,-1),$ and its maximum value is $16.$"} :step/id #uuid "b54506ce-0e0f-5fbb-b897-390b2885caf7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524064}}] :activity/title "Lagrange Multipliers With One Constraint" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523245}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531683} :activity/id #uuid "bb3101b4-4787-5bac-a3dd-30e1339ef299" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049390 :topic/id #uuid "f0e26f7e-2461-5845-b570-4028c1709f5a" :topic/math-academy-id 1948} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c067ceed-329e-51d4-8634-a1d0a81bb476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365228} {:answer/id #uuid "e75d0918-e0c0-5785-afab-223f2739e64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365229} {:answer/id #uuid "d89c552a-fc32-5beb-8d5d-4662a900a5fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365230} {:answer/id #uuid "3d19bfcb-0c7d-52e7-9dba-653ffcc18e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365231} {:answer/id #uuid "27d0d7f6-36e2-5084-93d4-3a3fe65c5a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365230} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bd3adff-ea86-59fd-bc52-90fe39fade8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c6ff3f5a-d486-56fd-99cb-379dcba19a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-162792" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ on the paraboloid ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-y=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y,z)$ exists on the surface.*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-y=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-1,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=-\\lambda \\\\ 2z &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1+\\lambda )x &=0 \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\lambda }{2} \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 1.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\ny={0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0,0).$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=0,$ but this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}={0}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{1}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "1cec84f6-1985-5992-8045-b17621c4fe6b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4770d7d9-2b84-5193-9e55-3f6a8c111bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361147} {:answer/id #uuid "50c2b9ea-27b8-5cd0-9059-6da07dd57ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361192} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1d655-a890-53be-a187-caa144dc19bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361573} {:answer/id #uuid "de8be4ac-d669-50ad-ba32-f79a71f23b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361829} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bff70f-6e8b-5be6-916a-ad154e650d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361949} :answer-field/id #uuid "34c90b2e-8911-5c08-acf8-00710f4117fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "67c90c79-df35-57ea-bcc9-2e5840258d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-99113" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+3y-z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=11\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3 &=2\\lambda y \\\\ -1 &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=11$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{11}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=\\pm 3,\\,z=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(1,3,-1),\\,(-1,-3,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,3,-1)$ | $(-1,-3,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $11$ | $-11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,-3,1),$ and its minimum value is $-11.$"} :step/id #uuid "369bfa0b-6a9e-5002-b279-fe1b70b8fbbc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c1a9567-a313-551f-8955-ecaba2978cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365215} {:answer/id #uuid "6db71774-d7fa-5f78-a1ef-b5912f624201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365216} {:answer/id #uuid "77dfb2d7-3549-503d-8180-ec857c5d2c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365217} {:answer/id #uuid "9899ec02-9b96-560f-a1cc-1d5024d0846e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365218} {:answer/id #uuid "40291836-4e30-51b2-ae65-ae6465fdf18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365219}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365218} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f4541b-0d8a-526a-afe2-a9aa2ea8fd81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c36493f2-f6b8-5f91-9602-a932566cf19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162765" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-6y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -6y &=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1+2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +3) &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-\\frac{1}{2}+3)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}=-\\frac{11}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,0)$ and its maximum value is $1.$"} :step/id #uuid "504a3430-1fcb-5302-9b1d-01fccb6cfb91" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "479580a8-ae4a-5bac-b57e-907136b973ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279232} {:answer/id #uuid "68331d3b-2043-56d0-817d-9396a163baef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279539} {:answer/id #uuid "adc6809c-7684-5cfd-9355-824085b4adfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280130} {:answer/id #uuid "c243fcc5-0d08-5788-ae18-34e5f70a626d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280300} {:answer/id #uuid "f1294da6-4be2-5e93-a3ff-774f95ee6a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280300} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0457b39-6e90-5602-a1db-a54071260e96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83f506ab-232e-59b7-85e6-ab9697a12cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162762" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y+4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+4 &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda -1) &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-1),(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2(1)-1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-1),\\,(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-1)$ | $(0,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,1)$ and its maximum value is $9.$"} :step/id #uuid "bdda49a4-b175-5f91-b4c7-25153ee6cd44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa485803-94c1-5d4d-81e7-ca9b8b7a3998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121494} {:answer/id #uuid "3a29822e-39a7-582e-aca5-5e12379238bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121495} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1be7dd-2737-5a69-b434-8b7b88c4b0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121496} {:answer/id #uuid "465acc67-9d0d-5fc3-8a3a-73ca3eeed040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121497} {:answer/id #uuid "55f4fa9d-ed39-532c-95b5-eb99e6d6f4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121497} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb06da8a-1bed-5b39-b00c-b31b265c8e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "794a3e0d-09de-59e6-90ac-90bc491d764c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162761" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the hyperbola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2(x-1),2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-1) &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y &=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=1 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1+1)=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the point $(1,0)$ and its minimum value is $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "e5502470-ff5a-5e08-b59d-88209eac09b9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531714}}] :activity/title "Lagrange Multipliers With One Constraint" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530863}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550086} :activity/id #uuid "c9f3da8d-5d00-57ac-88de-b31a901d6382" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049390 :topic/id #uuid "f0e26f7e-2461-5845-b570-4028c1709f5a" :topic/math-academy-id 1948} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f2ed59-53c9-5b2f-b846-8683f58bc76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279243} {:answer/id #uuid "babeccb9-a237-5cb0-82bc-c21e269e85fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280238} {:answer/id #uuid "c343408b-3b86-5652-aeee-97da743613e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280307} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1dcaea-8a35-5594-a4c9-8d44778d1d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280383} {:answer/id #uuid "ff19c910-670d-574b-b14d-f52081305537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280238} :answer-field/id #uuid "85fe8166-4c89-5f92-bcbe-bb29871f25b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "301d5a0d-2c92-53f7-94cf-b2627d84c6ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-162768" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1-2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2y &=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1+2\\lambda ) &=1 \\\\ y(1-4\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1+\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}),(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(-1,0)$ and its minimum value is $-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "41977640-49fb-5daa-8a1a-349f70fcd98b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53eff04f-9cf8-5a15-bf1e-3202677f56a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236352} {:answer/id #uuid "8deed6bb-9a7d-5a1d-97f3-9313fbc9e6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236715} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c66e0e-d6d3-51c4-80a3-331232d651f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236822} {:answer/id #uuid "f249a950-eb52-5a20-b5ef-cc54b4326e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237345} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c62bc3-8050-5b88-89ef-e157af90b11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236715} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9b86e15-3d6e-5aa9-80bd-3e76375a1e87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4df6773c-7996-5e96-920f-edaf9d38b1f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-101310" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3-y+5z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=26\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,-1,5⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1 &=2\\lambda y \\\\ 5 &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z &=\\frac{5}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=26$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =26 \\\\ 0+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ \\frac{26}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=∓1\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(0,-1,5),\\,(0,1,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-1,5)\\,$ | $\\,(0,1,-5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,29\\,$ | $\\,-23\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,1,-5),$ and its minimum value is $-23.$"} :step/id #uuid "4f98558b-0cc5-5e3d-9fba-b6e43c8b2518" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba6dc59a-d58e-52fa-ab73-eb12adb3b9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123255} {:answer/id #uuid "e0be39ec-ebe1-5490-8c20-efd23eff06dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123256} {:answer/id #uuid "0469e86f-4896-589f-be98-7d2dcb9b622a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123257} {:answer/id #uuid "66fdae75-3fd9-5769-80cc-77dba87e1ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123258} {:answer/id #uuid "72fceb0d-f6b0-5e5c-9ecb-2fc5b7e2e60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186123257} :answer-field/id #uuid "53789dcc-b6e4-5a59-b807-fa2867d7d6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4948ed57-4d3d-575d-8636-517fc5219397" :question/math-academy-id "q-37869" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+3y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=13\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=13$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,-3),\\,(2,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,-3)$ | $(2,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-13$ | $13$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,3),$ and its maximum value is $13.$"} :step/id #uuid "accda8d2-8c43-5f0a-b1ed-a8b823922d34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44296748-1b1f-55fb-b6be-3f1f679a363f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279202} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c0be99-daac-5fe1-aa90-0211c18e781e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280227} {:answer/id #uuid "c76ad7f1-b80d-5a93-af5f-ef37cbad1d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280339} {:answer/id #uuid "e72548ca-744f-5dba-81cb-314ca4deed0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280627} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2e7d8a-6624-5d7a-bde3-c06967f94904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280823} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ccfe924-d83d-5c3b-b4d2-db813f0c0a78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "046d11e3-1904-5ae7-b876-315526818d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162770" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,$ on the parabola ${y}^{2}-4x=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-4x=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-4,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=-4\\lambda \\\\ -4y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2\\lambda \\\\ y(\\lambda +2) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x & =0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-2(-2)\\,⇒\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(4,-4),(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,\\pm 4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,\\pm 4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(4,\\pm 4)$ and its minimum value is $-16.$"} :step/id #uuid "bae03328-2e4f-5cff-a2ca-b4b4e4a9f9df"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05dc044b-24e6-5b1f-8787-d56ca91a9176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278646} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0ba600-5352-5861-a7a7-22e72871086b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279690} {:answer/id #uuid "7e357422-bf28-5f1c-8a6c-e23d12be25da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279725} {:answer/id #uuid "b410e16c-dfd0-5424-90b9-d8368e817d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280177} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f2419e-a59e-59f5-8bb8-6f6008d18dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279690} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd67f28a-cbb2-587d-9c14-b9a7a306bc81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15a21e57-2278-5487-9586-a1ff33b69b24" :question/math-academy-id "q-162767" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y+4z$ on the ellipsoid $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=21\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=8\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y \\\\ 4 &=8\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{8\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=21$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =21 \\\\ 4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}})+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =21 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ \\frac{21}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=\\pm 2,\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},2,2),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},2,2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2),$ and its minimum value is $-\\frac{21}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "bbde05ab-2925-5669-b487-601868473480" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550048}}] :activity/title "Lagrange Multipliers With One Constraint" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549313}]}
